### 🟡 Issue 7 — No CORS Policy (A05)

Without an explicit CORS policy, browsers apply their default same-origin policy — but this becomes a problem the moment you add a frontend on a different domain, or if someone adds `cors()` without configuration (which opens the API to all origins).

**Recommendation:** Define an explicit allowlist now:
```js
const cors = require('cors');

const allowedOrigins = (process.env.ALLOWED_ORIGINS || '').split(',').filter(Boolean);

app.use(cors({
  origin: (origin, callback) => {
    // Allow requests with no origin (e.g. mobile apps, curl) only in dev
    if (!origin && process.env.NODE_ENV !== 'production') return callback(null, true);
    if (allowedOrigins.includes(origin)) return callback(null, true);
    callback(new Error(`CORS: origin '${origin}' not allowed`));
  },
  methods: ['GET', 'POST', 'PUT', 'DELETE'],
  allowedHeaders: ['Authorization', 'Content-Type'],
  credentials: true,
  maxAge: 86400,
}));
```

